## First run

Needs **Node <%= nodeFloor %>+** and **npm <%= npmFloor %>+**. On an older npm the
install-script allowlist under [Dependency policy](#dependency-policy) is
accepted and silently ignored, so the workspace looks gated and is not.

```bash
npm install
<% if (uiLib) { %>npm run build:libs   # before the first serve — see below
<% } %>npm start
```
<% if (uiLib) { %>
### Why `build:libs` comes first

Apps import libraries from `./dist/<lib>` — `tsconfig.json` maps each
library's name to its build output — not from source, and `ng serve` won't
build one on its own. So the first thing a fresh clone needs is:

```bash
npm run build:libs
```

Run it again any time a library's public API changes. Every `npm` script that
needs it runs it for you first, through a `pre*` hook — `npm start`,
`npm test`, `npm run build`, and the per-app `start:`, `build:`, `test:` and
`e2e:` scripts. Only `ng serve` and `ng test` invoked directly bypass the
hooks, so those are the two that need it by hand.

Working on a library and an app that consumes it at the same time? Run this
in the background to rebuild the library on every save:

```bash
npm run watch:libs
```

### Theming

Every app's `styles.scss` pulls in the design system's tokens, so every app
gets light and dark for free — a visitor's `prefers-color-scheme` decides,
in CSS, with nothing to wire up.

Apps generated with the `app` schematic go one step further: their shell
carries a theme toggle, and two attributes on `<html>` back it.

| Attribute | Values | Set by |
| --------- | ------ | ------ |
| `data-theme` | `light`, `dark`, or absent | The toggle. Absent means "follow the OS." |
| `data-palette` | A palette name | The toggle |

Every theme/palette combination is already declared in the stylesheet, so
switching either one is just writing an attribute — nothing re-renders, and
no component needs to know theming exists. A small inline script in
`index.html` applies the saved preference before the first paint, so you
never see a flash of the wrong theme.

Marketing sites have neither the toggle nor that script, on purpose: their
pages are prerendered once and served to everyone, so nothing may bake one
visitor's choice into the HTML.

To add a palette:

1. Declare it in `<%= libRoot %>/src/styles/_ref.scss`.
2. Add it to the `PALETTES` array in `<%= libRoot %>/src/lib/theme/theme.ts`.
3. Check both against WCAG, in light and dark:

   ```bash
   npm run check:contrast
   ```

   It fails if you only added the palette to one of the two places above.
