## Adding to this workspace

The generator stays installed, so the same schematics that built this
workspace also extend it:

```bash
ng generate angular-capacitor-workspace:app <name>          # a client-rendered app
ng generate angular-capacitor-workspace:marketing <name>    # a prerendered static site
ng generate angular-capacitor-workspace:ui-lib <name>       # a design-system library
ng generate angular-capacitor-workspace:mobile <app>        # a Capacitor shell for an app
ng generate angular-capacitor-workspace:codegen             # an OpenAPI client
```

Each one wires itself into the workspace it finds: new scripts land in the
table below, and anything that needs explaining is written into this file.

## Dependency policy

This workspace passed a security audit the day it was generated, and it ships
with the tools to keep passing.

```bash
npm run audit:policy          # resolve the lockfile, audit it, fail on anything new
npm run doctor                # show how you've drifted from the generator's current policy
npx angular-capacitor-workspace doctor --fix   # apply the fix
```

`package.json` carries two pieces of that policy:

- **`overrides`** — pins on transitive dependencies that fix known
  vulnerabilities with no upstream fix yet. Each one is explained in the
  generator's policy module.
- **`allowScripts`** — an allowlist (npm ≥ <%= npmFloor %>) for which dependencies may
  run install scripts. CI runs with `--strict-allow-scripts`, so a new
  dependency that wants to run one will fail the build until someone
  approves it.

To pick up new fixes after upgrading the generator, run `doctor --fix`.

## Scripts

Scripts are named `<verb>:<app>[:<platform>]`. Every time you generate
something new with `angular-capacitor-workspace`, it adds a row here for the
scripts it created, so this table always reflects what the workspace can
actually run. Rows you edit by hand are left alone.

